Title: Language robustness: NFC normalization and legacy-encoding sanity check
Created: 2026-09-01
Author: claude
Blocked-by: 0557

--- log ---
2026-09-01T13:31Z claude created

--- body ---
## Context

Child of tracker 0557. EN/FR/VN are MUST languages (author directive,
2026-09-01); the rest are SHOULD. Two real, silent failure modes surfaced
by the SOTA review's third pass:

1. PDF text extraction can return NFD-decomposed combining marks instead
   of NFC-precomposed glyphs, which breaks naive string/font comparison
   on any diacritic-heavy language (French, Vietnamese) unless normalized
   first.
2. Legacy 8-bit Vietnamese encodings (VNI, TCVN, VISCII), common in
   pre-Unicode Vietnamese PDFs, produce silently garbled text on
   extraction -- no library in the survey auto-detects this; it looks
   like successful extraction and isn't.

## Actions

1. Apply native `.normalize('NFC')` to every extracted string before any
   heading/byline pattern matching -- zero new dependency, fixes (1) for
   every Latin-script language including French and Vietnamese.
2. Build a garbled-text/legacy-encoding sanity check (e.g. a statistical
   check on the proportion of unexpected/control-range codepoints, or a
   basic per-language character-frequency plausibility check) that flags
   a document as extraction-unreliable rather than emitting garbage
   headings.
3. Route a flagged document into the existing confidence -> synthetic
   fallback pattern (ultimately seg/1's, per the ruling), not a silent
   pass-through.

## Test

- Fixtures: clean Unicode French and Vietnamese PDFs (should segment
  normally); an NFD-decomposed extraction (should normalize and segment
  correctly); a synthetic legacy-encoding-garbled fixture (should be
  flagged, not silently mis-segmented).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] NFC normalization applied at the single choke point every extracted
      string passes through
- [ ] legacy-encoding sanity check implemented and tested against a
      garbled fixture
- [ ] EN/FR/VN fixtures pass; a garbled fixture is flagged, not
      mis-segmented
